    overview_html: `<p>Shadowverse operates on a standard gacha model with monthly expansions. Players open packs using crystals (premium currency) or earned resources. The game guarantees legendary cards at specific intervals, though rates are less transparent than Western competitors. Shadowverse's unique class-based deck building (seven distinct classes) provides diversity, but acquiring competitive cards requires sustaining spending across all classes.</p><p>The game's Japanese roots mean cultural preferences differ from Western players—power creep is more aggressive, and monetization expectations are higher. A competitive collection costs roughly $80-150 per expansion, comparable to Hearthstone but with less developed free-to-play pathways.</p>`,
    pity_explanation_html: `<p>Shadowverse guarantees legendary within 75 packs on most banners. Rates vary by banner, which is less transparent than Hearthstone's consistent system. The pity system is functional but lacks the clarity of Western card games, requiring community documentation to fully understand.</p>`,
    history_html: `<p><strong>2016:</strong> Shadowverse launches in Japan with aggressive monetization typical of Japanese gacha games.</p><p><strong>2017:</strong> Western release brings temporary community friction over perceived predatory design compared to local expectations.</p><p><strong>2019:</strong> Free rewards expansion improves free-to-play viability after western player complaints.</p><p><strong>2021:</strong> Shadowverse Evolved mobile port launches, splitting development resources.</p><p><strong>2024:</strong> Shadowverse remains popular in Asia with primarily Asian monetization philosophy persisting.</p>`,
    controversy_html: `<p>The primary criticism involves cultural monetization differences. Japanese gacha games normalize higher spending than Western audiences expect, creating perception of unfairness. Additionally, Shadowverse's transparency lags peers—individual banner rates are documented by community rather than developer, creating trust issues. Power creep cycles force renetization every 2-3 months.</p>`,
